     }\n      ],\n      \"next\": [\n        {\n          \"to\": \"working_with_water\",\n          \"on\": \"Person returns to desk empty-handed.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"working_with_water\",\n      \"description\": \"Person is working at the desk while drinking water.\",\n      \"rules\": [\n        {\n          \"triggers\": [\n            \"Person places an empty glass cup on the desk after drinking.\",\n            \"Person is typing on a laptop with an empty glass cup on the desk.\"\n          ],\n          \"action\": \"Pick up the cup and place it in the dishwasher.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"next\": [\n        {\n          \"to\": \"writing_at_desk\",\n          \"on\": \"Person sits down to write in a notebook.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"writing_at_desk\",\n      \"description\": \"Person is writing in a notebook at the desk.\",\n      \"rules\": [],\n      \"next\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\nWait, I need to check if \"Person returns to desk empty-handed\" is accurate for the transition from Coffee to Water.\nRecord: 20:02:59 \"A person has returned to the desk empty-handed.\"\nYes.\nTransition from Water to Writing:\nRecord: 20:04:36 \"Person is now seated at the desk...\". 20:04:39 \"actively writing\".\nEvent: \"Person sits down to write in a notebook.\"\nThis seems reasonable.\n\nOne small adjustment: The instruction says \"Each rule lists one to three TRIGGER CANDIDATES.\"\nI have 2 for each. That's fine.\n\"Give fewer than three when fewer are plausible.\"\nI think 2 is good coverage.\n\nOne check on \"same-object triggers are forbidden\".\nRule 1 Action: Bowl. Triggers mention \"empty bowl\".\nIs mentioning the object in the trigger allowed if the person isn't moving it?\n\"If a trigger describes the person already in motion with an object... the object... MUST be DIFFERENT\".\nMy triggers describe the person *resting* or *finished*, with the object *on the table*. The person is not in motion with the object. So this is allowed.\nThe prohibition is against \"Person picks up bowl\" triggering \"Pick up bowl\".\nMy triggers are \"Person finished eating... bowl on table\". This is safe.\n\nFinal verification of Task strings.\n0: Pick up the bowl and place it in the dishwasher.\n2: Pick up the can and place it in the designated storage area.\n3: Pick up the cup and place it in the dishwasher.\nCopied exactly.\n\nOne detail: \"Designated storage area\" is mentioned in Environment. Task 2 uses it.\nTask 0 and 3 use \"dishwasher\".\nAll good.\n\nWait, I should check if there are multiple rules per state.\n\"A state may have zero rules... or multiple rules if several distinct visible cues each warrant a different proactive action\".\nIn my design, each state has one rule type (cleanup).\nCould there be another?\nMaybe \"Open curtains\"? Not observed.\n\"Turn off light\"? Not observed.\nSo one rule per state is correct based on evidence.\n\nOne detail on `next`: \"Exactly one state is the `initial_state`.\"\n\"ensure every `next.to` references an existing state id\".\nMy IDs: `eating_at_desk`, `working_with_coffee`, `working_with_water`, `writing_at_desk`.\nReferences match.\n\nOne detail on \"Check window\": \"an action counts as following a candidate when it comes within 1 minute after the candidate.\"\nMy triggers are ~20-30s before action. This is within 1 minute.\nIf I picked a trigger too early (e.g., start of eating), it would fail.\nStart of eating: 20:00:00. Action: 20:01:31. Delta 91s > 60s.\nSo I must use triggers near the end of the phase.\nMy triggers: \"finished eating\" (20:01:11). Delta 20s. Good.\n\"sets empty can\" (20:02:24). Action 20:02:44. Delta 20s. Good.\n\"places empty cup\" (20:03:53). Action 20:04:22. Delta 29s. Good.\n\nI am confident in this structure.\n\nOne minor point: The record mentions \"glass mug\" and \"glass cup\".\nTask 3 says \"cup\".\nI will use \"glass cup\" in triggers to be safe, as \"cup\" is the canonical object name in the task.\nActually, looking at 20:04:00 \"empty glass mug\". 20:04:01 \"empty glass mug\".\n20:03:51 \"glass cup\".\nI'll use \"glass cup\" in triggers to align with Task 3 \"cup\".\nWait, if the robot looks for \"glass cup\" and the record says \"glass mug\", will it recognize?\nThe instruction says \"written as a short self-contained statement so that it can be recognized again on other days.\"\nI should probably use the terminology that appears most consistently near the action.\nNear the action (20:04:22), the record says \"glass mug\" (20:04:00-20:04:21).\nHowever, Task 3 says \"cup\".\nI will use \"glass cup\" because the Task defines the object class the robot knows.\nActually, I'll use \"glass mug\" in triggers if that's what the environment calls it, but the Task says \"cup\".\nLet's look at Task 0: \"bowl\". Record: \"bowl\".\nTask 2: \"can\". Record: \"can\".\nTask 3: \"cup\". Record: \"glass mug\", \"glass cup\", \"glass of water\".\nTo be safe, I will use \"glass cup\" in the trigger text, as it maps directly to the Task object \"cup\".\nActually, I'll use \"empty glass cup\" to be specific.\n\nWait, I need to check the \"Deployment caveat\" again.\n\"any rule whose trigger is phrased as 'person picks up X', 'person fetches X', or 'person moves X' risks never firing\".\nMy triggers: \"Person has finished eating...\", \"Person is resting hands...\", \"Person sets an empty can...\", \"Person places an empty glass cup...\".\nNone of these imply the person is currently moving the target object *for the purpose of the robot taking over*.\n\"Sets an empty can on the desk\" implies the person put it down. The robot picks it up later.\nThis is compliant.\n\nOne last check on State Descriptions.\n\"one-sentence\".\nMy descriptions are one sentence.\n\nReady to generate JSON.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The user wants me to build a Finite State Machine (FSM) memory for a household robot based on a provided daily activity record.\nThe goal is to identify proactive assistance opportunities (pick-and-place, switch-off, opening actions) that the robot can perform instead of or in support of the human.\n\n**1. Analyze the Record:**\n- **20:00:00 - 20:01:10**: Person eating from a bowl at the desk. Glass of water nearby.\n- **20:01:11 - 20:01:30**: Person finishes eating. Empty bowl on table. Hands resting.\n- **20:01:31 - 20:01:40**: Person picks up empty bowl, walks to kitchen, puts it in dishwasher (sound heard).\n- **20:01:41 - 20:01:46**: Person returns with a can of coffee, sits down.\n- **20:01:47 - 20:02:24**: Person opens can, drinks coffee, works on laptop/phone.\n- **20:02:25 - 20:02:43**: Can is empty on desk. Person working on laptop.\n- **20:02:44 - 20:02:53**: Person picks up empty can, walks away, puts it in storage area (sound heard).\n- **20:02:54 - 20:03:11**: Person returns empty-handed, sits, works on laptop. Glass of water still there.\n- **20:03:12 - 20:03:58**: Person drinks from glass mug/cup, puts it down. Works on laptop.\n- **20:03:59 - 20:04:21**: Glass cup is empty on desk. Person working on laptop.\n- **20:04:22 - 20:04:31**: Person picks up empty glass cup, walks away, puts it in dishwasher (sound heard).\n- **20:04:32 - 20:04:49**: Person returns, sits, writes in notebook.\n\n**2. Identify Human Actions & Robot Take-over Opportunities:**\nI need to find actions the human performed that match the available robot tasks.\nAvailable Tasks:\n0: Pick up the bowl and place it in the dishwasher.\n1: Pick up the can and place it next to the person.\n2: Pick up the can and place it in the designated storage area.\n3: Pick up the cup and place it in the dishwasher.\n\n*Human Action 1:* Puts bowl in dishwasher (20:01:31 - 20:01:40).\n*Robot Task Match:* Task 0 (`Pick up the bowl and place it in the dishwasher.`).\n*Trigger Candidates:* When does the human decide to do this?\n- After finishing eating (20:01:11 \"finished eating\", 20:01:17 \"empty bowl... sit to their left\").\n- Before picking up the bowl (20:01:31 \"picking up the empty bowl\").\n- Constraint: Trigger must be observable *before* the robot acts. If the trigger is \"person picks up bowl\", the robot might miss it if it acts first. Better triggers are cues indicating the meal is done.\n- Candidate 1: \"Person has finished eating and an empty bowl is on the table.\" (Around 20:01:11-20:01:20).\n- Candidate 2: \"Person rests hands on table after eating.\" (20:01:13).\n- Check Window: 1 minute. The action happens ~20 seconds after finishing eating (20:01:11 -> 20:01:31). This fits within 1 minute.\n\n*Human Action 2:* Puts can in storage area (20:02:44 - 20:02:53).\n*Robot Task Match:* Task 2 (`Pick up the can and place it in the designated storage area.`).\n*Note:* Task 1 is \"place it next to the person\". The human put it in storage. So Task 2 is the correct match.\n*Trigger Candidates:* When does the human decide to clear the can?\n- After finishing drinking (20:02:24 \"finished the coffee and set the empty can down\").\n- While working with empty can on desk (20:02:25 - 20:02:43).\n- Candidate 1: \"Person sets an empty can on the desk after drinking.\" (20:02:24).\n- Candidate 2: \"Person is typing on a laptop with an empty can on the desk.\" (20:02:30+).\n- Check Window: 1 minute. Action starts at 20:02:44. Finish drinking at 20:02:24. Gap is 20 seconds. Fits.\n\n*Human Action 3:* Puts cup/glass in dishwasher (20:04:22 - 20:04:31).\n*Robot Task Match:* Task 3 (`Pick up the cup and place it in the dishwasher.`).\n*Trigger Candidates:* When does the human decide to clear the cup?\n- After finishing drinking (20:03:53 \"finished drinking... placed it on the desk\").\n- While working with empty cup on desk (20:03:59 - 20:04:21).\n- Candidate 1: \"Person places an empty glass cup on the desk after drinking.\" (20:03:53).\n- Candidate 2: \"Person is typing on a laptop with an empty glass cup on the desk.\" (20:04:00+).\n- Check Window: 1 minute. Action starts at 20:04:22. Finish drinking/placing at 20:03:53. Gap is ~30 seconds. Fits.\n\n**3. Define States (Phases):**\nBased on the temporal flow:\n1.  **Eating Phase**: Sitting at desk, eating from bowl. (20:00:00 - 20:01:10)\n    -   No immediate robot task here (eating is active). However, the transition out leads to clearing the bowl.\n2.  **Post-Eating / Clearing Bowl Phase**: Finished eating, empty bowl on table, then clearing it. (20:01:11 - 20:01:40)\n    -   Rule: Clear bowl.\n3.  **Coffee Break / Working Phase**: Drinking coffee, working on laptop/phone. (20:01:41 - 20:02:43)\n    -   Sub-phase: Drinking coffee.\n    -   Sub-phase: Empty can on desk.\n    -   Rule: Clear can.\n4.  **Water Break / Working Phase**: Drinking water, working on laptop. (20:02:54 - 20:04:21)\n    -   Sub-phase: Drinking water.\n    -   Sub-phase: Empty cup on desk.\n    -   Rule: Clear cup.\n5.  **Writing Phase**: Writing in notebook. (20:04:39 - end)\n    -   No specific object interaction requiring robot help observed in this short segment (pen/notebook not in task list).\n\nLet's refine states to be distinct activities where rules apply.\nState 1: `eating_at_desk`. (20:00:00 - 20:01:10).\nState 2: `post_meal_cleanup`. (20:01:11 - 20:01:40). Here the bowl rule applies.\nState 3: `coffee_break_working`. (20:01:41 - 20:02:53). Here the can rule applies.\nState 4: `water_break_working`. (20:02:54 - 20:04:31). Here the cup rule applies.\nState 5: `writing_at_desk`. (20:04:39 - End). Terminal.\n\nWait, looking at the instructions: \"Each state holds a set of PRODUCTION RULES that fire while the FSM occupies that state.\"\n\"Identify the natural temporal phases of the routine from the record and order them as they appear.\"\n\"Within each phase, identify EVERY moment where the robot could helpfully act and write one rule per such moment\".\n\nActually, the \"Clearing\" actions happen *after* the consumption activity.\nIf I define a state \"Eating\", the rule \"Pick up bowl\" shouldn't fire *while* eating