 (Finished eating, bowl present). Rules: Pick up bowl. Transition: \"Person leaves desk with bowl\".\nState 3: `drinking_coffee`. (Active drinking/working with full can). Rules: None. Transition: \"Person sets empty can down\".\nState 4: `can_on_table`. (Working with empty can). Rules: Pick up can. Transition: \"Person leaves desk with can\".\nState 5: `drinking_water`. (Active drinking/working with full cup). Rules: None. Transition: \"Person sets empty cup down\".\nState 6: `cup_on_table`. (Working with empty cup). Rules: Pick up cup. Transition: \"Person leaves desk with cup\".\nState 7: `writing_notebook`. (Writing). Rules: None. Terminal.\n\nThis seems granular but accurate to the \"trigger candidates\" requirement (observable moments before action).\nHowever, the instruction says \"Identify the natural temporal phases... Each state should correspond to a meaningfully different activity\".\n\"Eating\" and \"Finished Eating\" are arguably the same high-level activity \"Dining\". But the robot action depends on the state change.\nLet's consolidate slightly to avoid too many micro-states if possible, but ensure triggers work.\nIf State = `dining_phase`, it covers eating and post-eating.\nRule: \"If person finishes eating, pick up bowl\".\nTrigger: \"Person finishes eating\".\nAction: Pick up bowl.\nThis works.\nSimilarly for Coffee: `work_coffee_phase`. Rule: \"If person sets empty can down, pick up can\".\nSimilarly for Water: `work_water_phase`. Rule: \"If person sets empty cup down, pick up cup\".\n\nLet's check the constraint: \"Each rule lists one to three TRIGGER CANDIDATES... A moment further back than the check window always fails the check.\"\nCheck window is 1 minute.\nBowl: Finish eating (20:01:11) -> Pick up (20:01:31). Delta 20s. OK.\nCan: Set empty can (20:02:24) -> Pick up (20:02:44). Delta 20s. OK.\nCup: Set empty cup (20:03:53) -> Pick up (20:04:22). Delta 29s. OK.\n\nSo I can structure the states around the main activity blocks.\nState 1: `eating_at_desk`. Includes eating and the immediate aftermath before leaving.\nState 2: `working_with_coffee`. Includes drinking coffee and the aftermath before leaving.\nState 3: `working_with_water`. Includes drinking water and the aftermath before leaving.\nState 4: `writing_at_desk`.\n\nWait, the transitions need to be defined clearly.\nFrom `eating_at_desk` to `working_with_coffee`: Event \"Person returns to desk with can\".\nFrom `working_with_coffee` to `working_with_water`: Event \"Person returns to desk empty-handed\".\nFrom `working_with_water` to `writing_at_desk`: Event \"Person sits down to write\".\n\nLet's verify the triggers again.\nFor `eating_at_desk`:\nRule: Pick up bowl.\nTriggers:\n1. \"Person has finished eating and is looking ahead.\" (20:01:12)\n2. \"Person rests hands on table with empty bowl nearby.\" (20:01:14)\nThese occur before the robot would ideally act (within 1 min of human action).\nWait, if the robot acts, the human doesn't pick up the bowl. The trigger must be something the human does *regardless*.\n\"Person finishes eating\" is a good cue. \"Person rests hands\" is a good cue.\nConstraint: \"If a trigger describes the person already in motion with an object... the object... MUST be DIFFERENT\".\nHere the object is the bowl. Triggers like \"Person picks up bowl\" are forbidden because the robot should do it.\nSo triggers must describe the state *before* the human moves the bowl.\n\"Person has finished eating\" (20:01:11).\n\"Person is sitting at the desk with an empty bowl in front of them\" (20:01:17).\nThese are safe.\n\nFor `working_with_coffee`:\nRule: Pick up can.\nTriggers:\n1. \"Person sets an empty can on the desk after drinking.\" (20:02:24)\n2. \"Person is typing on a laptop with an empty can on the desk.\" (20:02:30)\nSafe.\n\nFor `working_with_water`:\nRule: Pick up cup.\nTriggers:\n1. \"Person places an empty glass cup on the desk after drinking.\" (20:03:53)\n2. \"Person is typing on a laptop with an empty glass cup on the desk.\" (20:04:00)\nSafe.\n\nOne detail: \"The memory will be used by the robot to proactively assist the person in future days\".\nIf I put the rule in the `eating_at_desk` state, it implies the robot watches for the trigger while in that state.\nOnce the trigger fires, the robot executes the action.\nDoes the state transition happen *after* the action? Or does the state transition happen when the human leaves?\nThe FSM describes the *human's* routine phases. The robot observes these phases.\nIf the robot performs the action, the human might not leave with the object.\nThe transition event \"Person leaves desk with bowl\" might not happen if the robot takes the bowl.\nHowever, the instructions say: \"Between states, EVENT-DRIVEN TRANSITIONS define progression... Transitions are not robot actions.\"\nAnd: \"Deployment caveat: ... any rule whose trigger is phrased as 'person picks up X'... risks never firing\".\nSo the transition events should also be robust.\nIf the robot clears the bowl, the human won't leave with the bowl.\nSo the transition from `eating_at_desk` to `working_with_coffee` should rely on the human returning with the *next* item (can), not the previous item (bowl).\nRecord: 20:01:37 \"Person has left the desk area...\". 20:01:44 \"Person has returned to the desk holding a can\".\nSo Transition: \"Person returns to desk holding a can\". This is robust even if robot cleared the bowl.\nSimilarly for Can -> Water: 20:02:59 \"Person has returned to the desk empty-handed\".\nSimilarly for Water -> Writing: 20:04:36 \"Person is now seated at the desk...\".\n\nOkay, let's finalize the states.\n1. `eating_at_desk`\n   - Description: Person is eating a meal at the desk.\n   - Rules:\n     - Trigger: \"Person has finished eating and an empty bowl is on the table.\"\n     - Trigger: \"Person is resting hands on the table with an empty bowl nearby.\"\n     - Action: Pick up the bowl and place it in the dishwasher.\n   - Next: To `working_with_coffee` on \"Person